//! run
//! abort
//! stderr: ft:19:5: runtime error: invalid release
//! stdout:
//| before
// A `del` of an owner that C gave is the runtime error `invalid release`: the address is no start
// of a live allocation of the runtime (D10.3, D10.9 rule 4). The C function that takes the owner
// as `own`, `free`, releases it.
// D10.3, D10.6, D10.9
import extern;
import std.libc;

// C call libc.calloc: no pointer argument; C gives 32 zero bytes or null.
fn main() i32 {
    void mut* own block = libc.calloc(1, 32);
    println("before");
    // The test needs storage of C: a null result has no address, and `del` of null does nothing.
    assert(block != null);
    del(block);
    println("after");
    return 0;
}
